.TH WAIT\-FOR 1 "2026-09" "init-toolkit" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wait-for \- wait for TCP and HTTP dependencies, then exec a command
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wait-for
.I TARGET
.RI [ TARGET ...]
.RB [ \-t
.IR SECONDS ]
.RB [ \-\-
.I command
.RI [ args ...]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
One front door for the readiness checks an init container keeps
re-implementing. Each TARGET is a URL:
.BI tcp:// host : port
targets are probed with netcat, and
.BI http:// ...
or
.BI https:// ...
targets must answer 2xx or 3xx.
.PP
When a command follows
.BR \-\- ,
it is exec'd once every target is ready, so this can be a container's
entrypoint without leaving a shell in the process tree.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-t " SECONDS"
Overall timeout (default 60). Exits non-zero if a target is still down.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every target became ready (or the exec'd command's own status).
.TP
.B non-zero
The timeout expired with a target still unreachable.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Wait for two dependencies, then start the app:
.PP
.RS
.B wait-for tcp://db:5432 tcp://redis:6379 \-t 90 \-\- ./app
.RE
.PP
Wait on a health endpoint:
.PP
.RS
.B wait-for http://api:8080/healthz
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR healthcheck-http (1),
.BR healthcheck-tcp (1),
.BR nc (1).
